@xyproto 

Although I agree with @mhx about the fact that DwarFS should be as simple as possible (thus no write option), I do think it can be useful for such Git repositories as you are describing.

However it's not straightforward but it can be done (these are not tested but based on my previous experiments should could work):
* **obviously I strongly suggest backing up things before this!**
* (optional) make sure your "base" repositories contain no unreferenced commits:
~~~~
git reflog expire --all --expire=all --expire-unreachable=all
git pack-refs --all

nice -n 19 -- git prune --expire=all --verbose
nice -n 19 -- git repack -A -d
nice -n 19 -- git prune --expire=all --verbose
nice -n 19 -- git fsck --root --tags --no-reflogs --full --connectivity-only --unreachable --dangling --name-objects
~~~~
* create an empty and "bare" git repository, let's call this "seed";
* find all `.pack` files from all your other repositories and call `git unpack-objects /absolute-path-to-other-git/objects/pack/something.pack` from within the "seed" repository;  (this will extract all objects, including commits, etc. in the shared "seed" git repository;)
* now your "seed" repository contains every object and commit all your other repositories contain;  (however it isn't useful for actual development;)
* (optional) before you proceed with DwarFS, try to see if `git repack -a -k` in the "seed" repository doesn't make things better;  make a backup of your "seed" repository first, or you'll have to unpack objects again;
* (optional) perhaps try to pack as above but instruct `git` not to compress anything;  also note that `git` has its own delta algorithm (I would perhaps disable that) and the option to limit the size of a pack file (I would also try to use this);  (without `.pack` files Git would have to access lots of commits and things could be quite slow just for this reason;)
* try to use DwarFS on the `.git/objects` folder from the "seed" repository;
* create empty new repositories for your original projects;
* use the `.git/objects/info/alternates` feature to point to the "seed" objects folder that you've just compressed with DwarFS;
* recreate the tags and branches (perhaps by doing a `git fetch /path-to-original-project/.git 'refs/*:refs/*'`);
* any new commits would just be created in each new repository, but objects from the "seed" would be reused from DwarFS;

As time passes, you can continue updating your DwarFS by importing new packs from other repositories and pruning them from the clones.

As such, perhaps if you apply the above DwarFS could be totally optional as things might just work without it.  (However DwarFS is useful for the fact that it provides a read-only file-system so it's quite difficult to destroy your "seed" repository.)

@rurban I would suggest switching to `Type=simple`, removing `RemainAfterExit` and running `dwarfs` with `-f` (for foreground) flag.

If that still fails, then perhaps add an `/usr/bin/strace -f` in front of `dwarfs` to see exactly what fails.

BTW, you can also use `systemd.mount` units (which are equivalent to `/etc/fstab` entries), but can have all the dependencies a normal service has.  (Using a `.service` unit type has the advantage of being able to configure various resource constraints, sandboxing, etc.)

@mhx I'll try to experiment with that branch at the end of this week.

However until then I've looked at the pointed documentation and I would suggest changing a little the names to better reflect what is happening behind the scenes:
* `-o no_image_madvise` based on what I understood it is my understanding that you've taken my default proposal of issuing a `madvise` with `MADV_DONTNED` by default;  so I would suggest renaming this one to `-o image_cache` to better reflect that using this the image would also be cached;  furthermore `madvise` is an implementation detail, and perhaps at some point you'll switch to a different implementation (like `O_DIRECT`) thus the flag will be inaccurate;
* `-o direct_io` is misleading because one could mistakenly understand that the image is read with `O_DIRECT`;  perhaps rename it to `-o no_fuse_cache` that reflects exactly what happens;  (`-o no_kernel_cache` would be an alternative but it's still unclear which files are cached by the kernel;)

In fact I would suggest also adding the complementary of each, i.e. `-o image_cache` vs `-o no_image_cache` and `-o fuse_cache` vs `-o no_fuse_cache`, this way one can always use the proper flag for once to make it clear (perhaps for documentation purposes in a script) what is used, and thus allowing you to change the defaults without affecting advanced users.

I understand that using an interpreted language opens many possibilities.  However I believe that a few common cases should be easily achieved through arguments.

Therefore given how important file permissions are (just like user / group IDs) I think there should be a few options dedicated to them.

----

For example I would look at how `rsync` handles this:
* there are options to set user / group ids (see `--chown` option);
* set permissions (see `--chmod` and `--executability` options);
* ignore empty directories, skip non-files;
* transform symlinks into their referents (thus eliminating the existance of symlinks especially given that you have support for deduplication;)
* etc.;

Indeed it fails due to the mismatch of `ld-linux-*.so`.

Additionally you need to do the following:
* use `readlink -e /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` to find it's real name;  (on my system it is `/lib64/ld-2.32.so`;)
* copy your `ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` on the target machine under the name `/usr/local/lib64/ld-2.32.so` (or anything else);
* use `patchelf --set-interpreter /usr/local/lib64/ld-2.32.so .../mkdwarfs`;

Then try to execute it.  For me it worked without an issue.

There is a minor issue with the `RPATH` in the executables:
* it must be `${ORIGIN}/../share/dwarfs/lib` (that is via `patchelf --set-rpath '${ORIGIN}/../share/dwarfs/lib' ./bin/mkdwarfs`);
* currently it is only `../share/dwarfs/lib` which doesn't work;

(The `${ORIGIN}` is verbatim.  This is the way it should actually appear when printed with `patchelf --print-rpath`.  It should be quoted or escaped so it's not touched by `cmake`, `make` or the shell.)

After patching the above, and using `patchelf --set-interpreter` to hard-code the `ld-2.31.so` that you've provided, it works on OpenSUSE Leap 15.2.

Regarding decompression, perhaps a somewhat different approach might fill this need but also open up the door for new possibilities.  How about this:  instead of a decompress feature that just provides a `rsync` alternative, what if there would be a `dwarfs2tar` or `dwarfs2cpio` tool that takes a DwarFS image and pipes to `stdout` the contents of that but in a format that would be understandable by `tar` or `cpio`.

By using such a tool someone could:
* (the main use-case) extract it to somewhere on the same machine;
* pipe it over `ssh` and extract it somewhere else on a different machine;
* provide a way to inspect the contents without actually extracting it;  (for example I've written a `md5-cpio` tool that can compute MD5 and the entire SHA family over a `cpio` archive without needing to extract it;)  basically anything else would fit in here;

Consequently a `cpio2dwarfs` or `tar2dwarfs` would be nice so that one can create `dwarfs` archives without actually having the files on a real file-system.

Thus one could easily migrate (or test) from one version of DwarFS to another one.

