As I read the original issue description, I think the requirements are as follows.

About the size limit, given that the target BD has a known maximum capacity, at least have a flag that instructs `mkdwarfs` to error out if that limit is passed.  A different (perhaps more involved) option would be to finalize creating the file-system image there, and start a fresh one with the remaining unprocessed files.  (I.e. `mkdwarfs` would split the files into multiple mutually exclusive data sets, and each resulting image would be self-contained and not relying on the others.  Then the user could use a union file-system if he wants everything back together.)

----

With regard the bit-rot protection, I think there are major advantages having that built into DwarFS, as for example:

* using any external solution (like `parchive`) implies that on any read error, one has to create a completely new image, thus in the case of a BD disk, burn out a completely new one, which unfortunately soon enough would have the same fate as the current one;

* using an external solution also means that any read error would block access to the affected files;  however if DwarFS would have built-in redundancy, it could just read the data from the recovery area, and move along without any down time;

* having a self-contained DwarFS image that contains both the data and the error correction means less hassle in archiving, moving and managing files;  which is especially important when handling physical medium like optical disks or HDD's;

----

That being said, for the second topic of bit-rot protection, perhaps a layered approach would work better, as for example:

* have a software solution that takes any file and provides a larger file including the bit-rot protection;  (in our case, the "file" is actually the original DwarFS image;)

* have another software that takes the larger (with bit-rot protection) file and presents the OS with the original file as a block-device;  (I think Linux has support for a FUSE-like solution but for block devices, if not the NBD protocol could be employed;  also if popular enough a device-mapper driver could be implemented to provide this;)

* just have DwarFS use that second mounted image, thus oblivious of the whole bit-rot protection features;

Such a solution, although somewhat more complex, could be used not only with DwarFS, but with any other (read-only) file-system out there.

In fact, the solution from `folly/folly/system/HardwareConcurrency.cpp` is great !

```
#include <folly/system/HardwareConcurrency.h>

#include <thread>

#include <folly/portability/Sched.h>

namespace folly {

unsigned int hardware_concurrency() noexcept {
#if defined(__linux__) && !defined(__ANDROID__)
  cpu_set_t cpuset;
  if (!sched_getaffinity(0, sizeof(cpuset), &cpuset)) {
    auto count = CPU_COUNT(&cpuset);
    if (count != 0) {
      return count;
    }
  }
#endif

  return std::thread::hardware_concurrency();
}

} // namespace folly
```

